4090871810913, "top": [[" or", -0.25914090871810913], [",", -2.1165971755981445]]}, {"t": " module", "lp": -0.3064867854118347, "top": [[" module", -0.3064867854118347], [" driver", -1.7743936777114868]]}, {"t": " options", "lp": -0.6737238764762878, "top": [[" options", -0.6737238764762878], [" parameters", -1.2479808330535889]]}, {"t": "),", "lp": -0.5677201747894287, "top": [["),", -0.5677201747894287], [" like", -1.4350945949554443]]}, {"t": " and", "lp": -0.02028048038482666, "top": [[" and", -0.02028048038482666], [" but", -4.014148235321045]]}, {"t": " its", "lp": -0.694221556186676, "top": [[" its", -0.694221556186676], [" the", -1.1490803956985474]]}, {"t": " precise", "lp": -0.2589316964149475, "top": [[" precise", -0.2589316964149475], [" behavior", -2.451201915740967]]}, {"t": " behavior", "lp": -0.26555943489074707, "top": [[" behavior", -0.26555943489074707], [" meaning", -1.634513258934021]]}, {"t": " has", "lp": -0.20289379358291626, "top": [[" has", -0.20289379358291626], [" depends", -2.2815966606140137]]}, {"t": " been", "lp": -0.5711992383003235, "top": [[" been", -0.5711992383003235], [" evolved", -0.8997228741645813]]}, {"t": " refined", "lp": -0.21993198990821838, "top": [[" refined", -0.21993198990821838], [" a", -2.646113872528076]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Behavior**: Uses a **dedicated CCS** (Compute Compute Slice) for each context that requests one.\n- Each context gets its own dedicated CCS, which can provide better isolation and performance for compute-heavy workloads.\n- More memory overhead since dedicated CCS is allocated per-context.\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **Behavior**: Uses a **shared CCS** that can be **time-sliced** across multiple contexts.\n- Multiple contexts share the same CCS resource.\n- More memory-efficient but may have contention when many contexts run simultaneously.\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- **Behavior**: Typically enables a **hybrid or fallback mode** that allows the driver to **dynamically choose** between dedicated and shared CCS allocation based on workload demands.\n- Useful when you want the driver to **automatically optimize** CCS usage without forcing a fixed strategy.\n\n## When to Use Each\n\n| Mode | Best For |\n|------|----------|\n| **1** | Compute-heavy workloads needing guaranteed dedicated resources; workloads where CCS contention causes performance issues |\n| **2** | General use; memory-constrained scenarios; workloads with many concurrent contexts |\n| **4** | Mixed workloads; when you want automatic optimization; when you're unsure which static mode fits your use case |\n\n---\n\n**A note on accuracy:** The exact semantics of these modes can vary between driver versions, and I want to be careful not to give you definitively wrong information. The `ccs_mode` parameter is passed to the Xe driver (often via kernel command line or module options), and its precise behavior has been refined"}